/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349451} {:answer/id #uuid "9af31c0d-f620-5346-8cc8-0520d3750ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349828} {:answer/id #uuid "d7bf9bfb-32be-5f38-ad4d-61f15bcaba54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350336} {:answer/id #uuid "e73a838e-f880-5ec9-8ca6-4f2bee27ab24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350469} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ff9120-1464-5615-9c5e-ff1e12550f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d08356c-7931-5cd9-9d11-4c39181be760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349451} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf5d9a8-e075-5599-8957-df3a4a61095c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a8774bf-f531-5684-a4ee-be071e1e5ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-82599" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=3{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=3{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1e14eb-c6fe-5d5e-a0dc-b061059559c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394416} {:answer/id #uuid "985b430f-4b57-5849-b48f-23adc660de97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394417} {:answer/id #uuid "dab5b566-c168-514d-b3c6-1c83c5b47cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394418} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a4ab04-a137-58d3-a387-4f383d0ae878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394419} {:answer/id #uuid "71cfe1b4-e7cb-5edf-9ce0-5c9a0fe890fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985b430f-4b57-5849-b48f-23adc660de97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394417} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7cbaef8-14a8-5424-bff9-cdd4ca81b618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c7d63da-59ab-5308-9b89-bf650fb9db9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82606" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84bf4f13-8394-5e74-8611-52bcbe63b443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394423} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f43405-b85b-5f16-ae6f-5e4abfc15221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394424} {:answer/id #uuid "1be8e3f6-f794-5e2a-9dc7-026e640b732f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394425} {:answer/id #uuid "924d16bd-723c-52e2-9669-dce0fa52dce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394426} {:answer/id #uuid "80c96893-628f-5b23-b717-b15529348c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924d16bd-723c-52e2-9669-dce0fa52dce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394426} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc08976-f574-52b6-a28f-57c50285b1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13aef9df-8682-5fe0-a906-e0817933e236" :question/math-academy-id "q-82609" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a356bf6-c5f6-5626-b37d-d3aa5d04ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349277} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1ff8f8-bc39-524a-8272-e771ca3767b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349849} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4fcc3c-344a-5fe2-a3f1-717dc5d18898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350145} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcd859b-049f-5e51-885f-b10217b48fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350241} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9f3522-d091-5f2a-a8c4-a46fc74e67fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a356bf6-c5f6-5626-b37d-d3aa5d04ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349277} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa41aa0-25a9-5a0a-b9d6-671a033593b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77f1fca8-2c83-502e-92f3-c4632be6ced3" :question/math-academy-id "q-82618" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 18 & -29 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :questio