10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
